Context Window in LLMs

When interacting with an LLM, it has a context window, meaning it can only process a limited number of tokens at one time (input + output combined).

If the conversation becomes longer than that limit:

Older tokens/messages may be truncated
The model may lose earlier context
It can appear to “forget” previous parts of the conversation

So memory systems are introduced to reduce this problem.



Types of Memory in LLM Applications
1. Short-Term Memory

This is memory used within the active conversation/session.

It usually includes:

Recent chat history
Current task details
Messages still inside the context window

2. Long-Term Memory

This stores important information outside the context window and retrieves it when needed later.

Usually implemented using:

Vector databases
User profile memory
Persistent storage
RAG systems

Types of LongTerm Memory
Semantic   → facts
Episodic   → past chats/actions
Procedural → how to do things
Preference → personalization
Project    → ongoing work



 Mem0 and Zep provide production-ready external memory solutions. Mem0 offers a managed memory layer that automatically extracts, stores, and retrieves relevant information. 





Problems While Using LTM in LLMs
1. Incorrect Memory Storage

Wrong or low-quality information may be saved.

Example:
User jokes “I am CEO of OpenAI” and system stores it as fact.

Result:

Future responses become inaccurate.

2. Retrieval Errors

Relevant memory may not be fetched, or irrelevant memory may be retrieved.

Example:
User asks Python debugging help, but system retrieves travel preferences.

Result:

Bad context, weaker answers.

3. Memory Conflicts / Contradictions

Stored facts can change over time.

Example:

Old memory: user lives in Nagpur
New memory: user moved to Pune
Result:

Conflicting responses unless updated correctly.

Mem0 saves  memory into these types all by itself
It is designed to automatically handle memory extraction, storage, updating, and retrieval for LLM apps so you don’t have to manually code all the memory logic.


